p.src:
Choose any one probability. The lecture chooses $p_7$, partly because $1$ looks too special and $7$ is a lucky number. Nothing depends on that choice. The only calculus required is $\frac{d}{dp_i}\bigl(p_i\log p_i\bigr)=\log p_i+1.$ Thus $\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial p_i}
 =\log p_i+1+\alpha+\beta E_i=0.$ Solving for $p_i$, $\begin{aligned}\log p_i&=-(1+\alpha)-\beta E_i,\\
 p_i&=e^{-(1+\alpha)}e^{-\beta E_i}.\end{aligned}$ The first factor is independent of the state label. Write it as $1/Z$: $p_i=\frac{e^{-\beta E_i}}{Z}.$

